# Comments

Comments should explain things the code can't. If a sentence repeats
the function name, restates the type, or paraphrases prose that already
lives in [`docs/`](../../docs), delete it.

## Rules

- **No JSDoc on small / self-evident helpers.** A good name plus a
  type signature is enough. Reserve doc-blocks for genuinely
  surprising contracts (race handling, ordering guarantees, throw
  conditions that aren't obvious from types).
- **Don't duplicate `docs/`.** Architecture, lifecycle, and public-shape
  prose live in [`docs/`](../../docs) — see
  [`architecture-docs.mdc`](./architecture-docs.mdc). The matching
  source file gets a one-line pointer at most. If you find yourself
  writing a paragraph in a `.ts` file, the paragraph belongs in the
  doc.
- **No narrative blocks restating the next few lines.** "Build X
  because Y" comments tend to paraphrase the code below them. Trust
  the code. Inline a short *why* only when a reader would otherwise
  ask "is this a bug?".
- **No field-level prose** unless the field carries a non-obvious
  invariant. The type and the name already say what it is.
- **Keep the comments that survive short.** One or two lines. If it's
  longer, it's probably a doc.

## ❌ Bad

```ts
/**
 * Whether `el` is currently claimed by `mountView` (in flight or
 * fully mounted). Used by the registry's mount path to dedup and by
 * the descendant walk to stop at view boundaries.
 */
export function isView(el: Element): boolean {
  return views.has(el);
}

// tag name -> mount fn. Throws on collision.
readonly #viewsByTag = new Map<string, MountFn>();

// Top-down barrier: wait for the closest ancestor view to finish
// mounting before this view does anything observable. Ancestor
// failure propagates as the promise rejection — descendants stay
// un-mounted in that subtree.
const ancestor = findAncestorMounted(el);
if (ancestor) await ancestor;
```

## ✅ Good

```ts
export function isView(el: Element): boolean {
  return views.has(el);
}

readonly #viewsByTag = new Map<string, MountFn>();

const ancestor = findAncestorMounted(el);
if (ancestor) await ancestor;
```

## ✅ Worth keeping

A comment that names a non-obvious *why* with a concrete trap:

```ts
// Re-derive the canonical path after exports resolution: asking for
// "." may have landed on "dist/index.js"; relative imports inside
// should resolve against "dist/", not the requested subpath.
const canonicalPath = canonicalPathOf(folderHandle, fileHandle, path);
```

When in doubt, cut prose and add a link to the source or a `docs/`
page instead.
